3h51|n|One who entertains for another such sentiments of esteem, respect, and affection that he seeks his society aud welfare; a wellwisher; an intimate associate; sometimes, an attendant. ; One not inimical or hostile; one not a foe or enemy; also, one of the same nation, party, kin, etc., whose friendly feelings may be assumed. The word is some times used as a term of friendly address. ; One who looks propitiously on a cause, an institution, a project, and the like; a favorer; a promoter; as, a friend to commerce, to poetry, to an institution. ; One of a religious sect characterized by disuse of outward rites and an ordained ministry, by simplicity of dress and speech, and esp. by opposition to war and a desire to live at peace with all men. They are popularly called Quakers. ; A paramour of either sex.	a:vve,94,32;a8bd,92,6;27tp,76,26;5olr,71,2;3j3r,70,39;884a,64,3;27tk,63,1062;58xk,61,27;1nr5,60,7;5ma,58,27;62t6,58,453;20mx,58,8;7rl1,57,2;ijd,57,6;5dz0,54,12~vu6,38,6287;3sqe,18,3004;6lfq,27,1795;1l1x,25,1521;27tk,63,1062;96ey,25,590;62t6,58,453;37qm,43,317;1h3p,33,274;4rj5,36,272;5ion,37,222;bob,27,210;71ra,16,206;4cha,35,190;1l68,24,163	v:642u,58,2;9tug,48,3;1pl8,47,12;1fbj,45,13;8anb,44,2;2w9s,44,12;27tk,43,250;377k,43,4;vgv,38,1;4d85,38,12;9vm0,37,202;307f,36,4;7vx5,36,5;707e,35,162;3gtx,33,4~8goq,1,262;27tk,43,250;9vm0,37,202;707e,35,162;7hnv,4,135;28if,7,84;6su2,19,23;8qpu,24,22;9h7g,30,21;adj3,10,17;4m2k,13,16;4m2m,19,14;1fbj,45,13;2891,30,12;2w9s,44,12	V:4szm,79,9;64yk,79,2;6fsj,77,2;7buy,76,4;6epy,72,2;aa79,70,3;68li,69,9;9jhj,65,17;3fgq,64,2;2q0t,62,2;1rai,55,3;4r8s,55,14;hcf,54,35;5c7e,54,16;9b4q,53,12~9pyq,51,2502;888b,21,1532;kmm,27,1139;98ii,36,1135;3l15,33,911;8l3e,30,753;8arb,13,723;3t4j,4,616;ag6t,29,562;aebg,0,548;18lm,19,509;789p,31,501;8h3f,20,436;3p6h,5,341;66d4,16,332	n:3mp7,85,8;12iw,79,10;1gvp,77,443;2m46,67,10;edn,67,2;2s43,67,4;5nyi,66,12;642u,64,3;1cw7,64,4;27to,63,5;6g07,62,9;5olr,61,1;2zlo,56,5;3ml5,55,7;5gq8,54,6~353i,35,783;1gvp,77,